# Membrane Separation in Organic Liquid: Technologies, Achievements, and Opportunities

## abstract
Membrane technology is one of the most promising technologies for separation and purification that is routinely and commercially employed in aqueous solutions. In comparison, its applications in organic solvents are severely underdeveloped mainly due to the poor stability of traditional polymer membranes in organic solvents. The emerging materials such as crosslinked polymers, covalent organic frameworks, metal-organic frameworks, conjugated microporous polymers, carbon molecular sieves, and graphene provide the solutions to address this problem. The membranes constructed with these novel materials show outstanding separation performance in regard to both high selectivity and solvent permeability, greatly pushing forward utilization of membrane technology in organic media. Here, an overview of the most important organic mixtures that need to be separated, the major separation processes adopted nowadays in organic solvents, and the recent progress in new developed membranes is provided.

Membrane technology is one of the most promising technologies for separation and purification that is routinely and commercially employed in aqueous solutions. In comparison, its applications in organic solvents are severely underdeveloped mainly due to the poor stability of traditional polymer membranes in organic solvents. The emerging materials such as crosslinked polymers, covalent organic frameworks, metal–organic frameworks, conjugated microporous polymers, carbon molecular sieves, and graphene provide the solutions to address this problem. The membranes constructed with these novel materials show outstanding separation performance in regard to both high selectivity and solvent permeability, greatly pushing forward utilization of membrane technology in organic media. Here, an overview of the most important organic mixtures that need to be separated, the major separation processes adopted nowadays in organic solvents, and the recent progress in new developed membranes is provided.
## 1. Introduction
Membrane separation is a technique in which pressure difference is applied on both sides of a membrane to allow the components to selectively pass through the membrane and get separated.[3] Since the late 1960s, membrane technology has been widely used for separation and purification owing to its low energy consumption, reduced operation time, good safety, and environmental compatibility when compared with conventional thermal-based evaporation and distillation. This technology is now very popular in water purification and seawater desalination or gas separation with a dense polymer membrane as the separating layer.[4] However, the application of membrane technology in organic system lags behind mainly owing to the poor solvent stability of conventional polymers. Learning the lessons from aqueous solution, recent developments in advanced materials have enabled fabrication of chemically stable membranes with outstanding molecular selectivity and permeance, extending the application of membrane separation into more aggressive organic media.[5]
Here, we first introduce several crucial separations in organic solvents, and the gas- or aqueous solution-based separations is intentionally excluded since they have been comprehensively reviewed.[6] Next, veral typical separation processes that are broadly adopted in ganic system including pervaporation (PV), organic solvent nanofiltration (OSN), organic solvent reverse osmosis (OSRO), and organic solvent forward osmosis (OSFO) have been briefly introduced. Finally, we summarize the significant recent advances in new material design and membrane construction, ying particular attentions to the state-of-the-art researches on organic separation. Note that some examples related to gas or aqueous solution separation might be included because the used materials might be also good candidates for future application in organic separation.
## 2. Important Separation System
In 2016, Sholl and Lively[2] summarized seven chemical separations that would change the word, including hydrocarbons from crude oil, uranium from sea water, alkenes from alkanes, greenhouse gases from diluted emissions, rare earth metals from ores, benzene derivatives from each other, and trace contaminates from water. All these examples represent the crucial mixtures in various media (aqueous, organic, and gaseous) that need to be separated. Compared with well-developed separation processes in aqueous solutions (like water treatment) and gas (like air separation and olefin/paraffin separation), separations in organic liquids have yet to be extensively reported and utilized because the more aggressive and complex organic mixtures impose the stringent requirements on the stability of the membrane. From the viewpoint of separations in organic liquids, several representative mixtures could be benefited from membrane-based technology, and we generally classify them into solute–solvent separation and solvent–solvent separation based on the difference in molecular size.
## 2.1. Solute–Solvent Separation
Removal of the large molecules from organic solvents is highly required to either concentrate the high-value solutes (solute enrichment) or discard the dissolved impurities (solvent recovery).[7] In this case, the “solvents” and “solutes” are clearly defined due to the big difference in molecule size (solutes are usually more than 10 times larger than solvents). The solutes are rejected by the membrane while the solvents pass through, leading to enrichment of solutes or purification of solvents. Representative examples include: 1) concentration and isolation of antibiotics, pharmaceutical intermediates, or peptides from organic solvents; 2) separation of natural compounds with biological activity such as vitamins, antioxidants, flavonoids, terpenoids, carotenoids, catechins, phytoestrogens, and minerals; and 3) solvent recovery in pharmaceutical, consumer chemical, and specialty chemical industries.[8]
## 2.2. Solvent–Solvent Separation
In sharp contrast to solute–solvent separation with distinct difference in size, solvent–solvent separation places much greater demands on the structural regulation of membranes since the size difference of different solvents is very small. In this part, we introduce several crucial but highly challenging organic solvent separations.
## 2.2.1. Xylene Isomer Separation
The xylene isomers, namely, $p$ -xylene, m-xylene, and $o$ -xylene, with two methyl groups at different positions of a benzene ring are mostly generated from the catalytic reforming of crude oils.[9] Each isomer acts as the valuable intermediate with broad applications in chemical industry. Among these, $p$ -xylene is the most profitable as the raw material for synthesizing polyethylene terephthalate (PET) and polybutylene terephthalate (PBT). $\textit{o}$ -Xylene is largely used in production of phthalic anhydride, while $m$ -xylene is employed for preparing isophthalic acid. Due to the importance of xylene isomers, efficient separation of them has been a long-lasting hot topic. Nevertheless, the structural similarities and the close boiling point make separation and purification of xylene monomers very difficult, for instance, the classic distillation method is not applicable. The current separation of these isomers is based on the difference in adsorption characteristic and freezing point (referring to adsorption and fractional crystallization), dealing with $75\%$ and $25\%$ of xylene isomer separation, respectively.[10] As for the adsorption method, various absorbents have been adopted such as zeolites, carbon materials, polymers, silicon gels, and metal–organic frameworks (MOFs).[11] In addition to adsorption, membrane technology shows great potential for xylene separation. Most recently, organic solvent reverse osmosis was used to separate xylene
