## 2.2.2. Alkane Isomer Separation
The production of gasoline benefits a lot from separation of alkane isomers, which is extremely challenging due to their similar physiochemical properties. Generally, the molecular size and shape are the origin for their differentiation, for example, hexane of formula $\mathrm{C_{6}H_{14}}$ is composed of five isomers with different research octane number (RON), and the content of each isomer is $10{-}30\%$ . The most branched 2,2-dimethylbutane and 2,3-dimethylbutane possess the highest RON values of 105 and 94, respectively,[13] while the RON values of less branched 2-methylpentane and 3-methylpentane are substantially lower to be 74 and 75, respectively. The linear hexane has the lowest value of 30. Therefore, the linear hexane must be separated to achieve the blends of four branched isomers with relatively higher RON value. Currently, more than two million barrels of pentanes and hexanes need to be processed daily all over the world, and the major strategy to separate linear hexane from branched isomers is to sieve the linear hexane by zeolite.
