## 2.2.4. Other Organic Solvent Mixtures
Thanks to the importance in chemical and petrochemical industries, the separation of organic/organic mixtures has been extensively studied, e.g., polar/nonpolar mixtures, aromatic/ alicyclic mixtures, aromatic/aliphatic mixtures, and aromatic isomers (this part has been discussed in Section  2.2.1).[14,18] As a typical representative of polar and nonpolar mixtures, the separation of alcohol/alkane and alcohol/ether mixtures, which involves separation of $n$ -hexane, $n$ -heptane, methyl tert-butyl ether (MTBE), and ethyl tert-butyl ether (ETBE), is adopted to achieve the antiknock replacement for tetraethyl lead to improve RON value of gasoline.[19] Besides, the separation of aromatic compounds from alicyclic compounds and other aromatics has been studied since the early 1960s. Among them, benzene/cyclohexane is the most common mixture. As an important industrial chemical, cyclohexane may be produced by catalytic hydrogenation of benzene. To obtain the pure cyclohexane, the unreacted benzene must be removed from the discharge stream of the reactor. It is extremely difficult to separate benzene/cyclohexane effectively by conventional distillation method since their boiling point and molecular volume are close. Thus, separation of benzene and cyclohexane is believed to be one of the biggest challenges in the chemical industry.[20] Finally, separation of alkanes and aromatics is another important topic. The first organic/organic solvent separation task proposed by the refiners is to reduce the benzene content of C6 reformates used in production of premium leadless gasoline. The latest American Environmental Protection Agency’s regulation (MSAT II) requires the refineries to reduce aromatic hydrocarbons to less than $0.62\mathrm{\vol}\%$ in gasoline, making this separation even more important.[21]
## 3. Typical Membrane Separation Process
Several classical processes have been explored for organic separations, which is briefly summarized and compared in this part based on their characteristics, advantages, and limitations. In addition, the opportunities on the future research is also discussed.
## 3.1. Pervaporation
Pervaporation is a membrane separation strategy for liquid mixture in which the feed is partial vaporized through a membrane, resulting in collection of permeating components in vapor form.[22] This vaporized component is removed by flowing an inert medium or applying low pressure on the permeate side (Figure  1a). The driving force of pervaporation is the difference in chemical potential, that is, the concentration gradient between phases on the opposite sides of a membrane. The mass transport is described by a sorption–diffusion model based on the affinity difference rather than the volatility difference in the traditional distillation process. It must be pointed out that phase change is involved in the pervaporation process, but we still consider it to be liquid phase separation here because the feed mixture is liquid.[23] In the past two decades, pervaporation has found its broad applications in liquid hydrocarbon separation, removal of volatile organic compounds from water, elimination of water from glycerin, and dehydration to intensify esterification reaction.[24] Nevertheless, phase change still exists in pervaporation process, consuming the similar amount of energy to that in distillation or evaporation.
## 3.2. Organic Solvent Nanofiltration
OSN refers to the solvent/solute separation that typically sieves the organic molecules with molecular weight of $200{-}1000\ \mathrm{g\mol^{-1}}$ (Figure 1b).[25] As an emerging area of membrane science and technology with its first commercial success in the early 1990s, OSN is at the intersection of pressure-driven filtration and chemical potential–driven separation, so the mass transport can be described by either the sorption–diffusion or the pore-flow model.[26] Unlike aqueous nanofiltration, which relies on both the steric and electrostatic interaction synergy, OSN is solely based on the physical size exclusion effect and imposes stringent requirements that are hardly satisfied with current polymers. In detail, the membranes must possess pores below $2\ \mathrm{nm}$ to reject solutes with molecular weight less than $1000\ \mathrm{g\mol^{-1}}$ and simultaneously have the remarkable stability and high permeance in a wide range of organic solvents with different polarities. Owing to the relatively loose structures and lager pores in OSN membranes, the operation pressure in nanofiltration is lower than that of reverse osmosis while higher solvent flux might be achieved. In the past several years, the limit of OSN membrane flux is largely pushed forward by creating extremely thin membrane (less than $10\ \mathrm{nm}$ ) or enhancing the microporosity using rigid chemical structures.[27] However, the rather low selectivity of OSN membrane restricts their application in finer separations, i.e., separation of organic molecules with molecular weight lower than $100~\mathrm{Da}$ .
## 3.3. Organic Solvent Reverse Osmosis
Leaning lessons from the sea water desalination by reverse osmosis, OSRO has been developed to discriminate the organic molecules with subtle differences in molecular sizes (less than $1-3\mathrm{~\AA})$ or physiochemical properties, such as isomers.[28] Different from OSN process that is usually used to separate large solutes from solvent, the OSRO process can acquire much higher selectivity because the pore size of OSRO membrane is considerably smaller (usually less than $1\ \mathrm{nm}$ ) and separation mechanism is primarily based on small differences in kinetic diameters (Figure 1c). Similar with their aqueous counterpart,
OSRO processes are best described by the sorption–diffusion model with chemical potential as the main driving force. The OSRO process is still in its very infancy though the original concept was demonstrated by Souriranjan in 1970 using the integrally skinned asymmetric cellulose acetate membrane to separate alcohols from cyclic hydrocarbons and alkyl aromatics.[19b,29] Little improvements have been achieved afterward until the “entropic selectivity” in carbon molecular sieve (CMS) membrane was recently reported and successfully used for xylene isomer separation with diffusion selectivity up to 30 and separation factor of 4.5 for para- and ortho-xylene isomers.[12]
## 3.4. Organic Solvent Forward Osmosis
OSFO is a membrane process in which a “draw” solution on the downstream side is used to create a transmembrane osmotic pressure gradient and extract the target molecules from the upstream (Figure  1d).[30] It has been widely used in the last decade for water purification and sea water desalination. Unlike the conventional pressure-driven membrane processes such as reverse osmosis (RO) and nanofiltration (NF) that employ external hydraulic pressure, forward osmosis (FO) takes the advantage of chemical gradient across a semipermeable membrane to transport water from the low concentration side to the high concentration side. Following the same principle, OSFO can simultaneously concentrate the pharmaceutical products in the feed while transporting the organic solvent to the draw solution. Subsequently, the diluted draw solution may be regenerated by filtration, distillation, or evaporation. Since no external pressure is needed, OSFO offers a number of advantages over the pressure-driven processes, such as lower fouling tendency and higher flux recovery, which would lower the operation and maintenance costs.[31] In addition, OSFO is expected to be utilized in the challenging separations where the osmotic pressure of solvent is too high to be operated in the pressuredriven process such as OSN and OSRO. Despite the advantages of OSFO process, it needs to be noted that additional steps are required to regenerate the draw solution. Previous researches on OSFO are mainly focused on its application in breaking distillation azeotropes, and seldom work has been carried out for size and shape discrimination.
## 4. Materials for Membrane Separation
## 4.1. Carbon-Based Materials
As a classic material, the porous carbon has been extensively used in absorption or separation fields since the last century due to their easy acquisition and excellent stability. Specially, porous carbon molecular sieves and diamond-like carbon (DLC) have been recently employed for separations in organic liquid. In addition, graphene-based materials, a special type of carbon, are also adopted.
## 4.1.1. Carbon Molecular Sieves
CMSs are usually made by pyrolysis of organic precursors such as amorphous polymers. Once carbonized, the CMSs composed of rigid slit-like ultramicropores become much more rigid and less swelling than their polymer precursors.[32] Although the surface area of CMSs is relatively low, their pore size is as small as several angstroms with narrow distribution, enabling molecular level separation caused by the size and shape discrimination. Traditionally, CMSs have been widely used in gas separation such as olefin/paraffin, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/$ $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ , etc.[33] Most recently, CMS membranes also show their great potential for separation in organic solvents. Lively and coworkers[12] reported a self-standing CMS membrane by using the crosslinked poly(vinylidene difluoride) (PVDF) hollow fiber membrane as precursor (Figure 2). After pyrolysis, the porous structure of PVDF membranes was well preserved, leading to a CMS hollow fiber membrane. As-prepared CMS membrane exhibited 10 times higher xylene isomer fluxes compared with the zeolite membranes, simultaneously, the excellent selectivity toward the benzene derivatives with molecular size difference less than $0.1\mathrm{nm}$ was achieved. As a result, the effective separation with high selectivity was realized at ambient temperature in the para- and ortho-xylene liquid mixtures. This work provides a promising candidate for organic solvent separation as well as opens a door for energy-efficient separation of aromatic isomers.
## 4.1.2. Diamond-Like Carbon
DLC is a class of amorphous carbon that displays some of the typical properties of diamond and is synthesized by plasma chemical vapor deposition or ion-beam deposition.[34] With the deposition technique, thin transparent DLC membranes were prepared by Aisenberg and Chabot in 1971.[35] The existing
DLC consists of highly 3D crosslinked networks of $\displaystyle\mathrm{sp}^{3}$ carbon and $\mathrm{sp}^{2}$ sites. The structure of DLC membranes can be adjusted by using different precursors and deposition parameters, while the attractive properties such as optical, electrical, mechanical, and chemical properties are also tuned.[36] Therefore, the application of DLC membrane for $\mathrm{OSN}$ has attracted much attention. Karan et  al.[37] fabricated an ultrathin $35\ \mathrm{nm}$ DLC membrane on a porous alumina support by using a parallelplate plasma-enhanced chemical vapor deposition technique (Figure  3). By altering the organic precursors and deposition parameters, the pore size and mechanical property were tuned accurately. The separation performance of DLC membranes was tested and exhibited the excellent permeation for different organic solvents. The hexane flux was up to $\approx390\ensuremath{\mathrm{~L~m~}^{-2}\mathrm{~h~}^{-1}}$ , which was 3 orders of magnitude higher than the commercially available membranes. Moreover, the DLC membranes showed high mechanical strength with Young’s moduli of $90{-}170\ \mathrm{GPa}$ , highlighting that the DLC membrane possessed excellent mechanical strength and might be used under harsh conditions like high-pressure separation.
## 4.1.3. Graphene Oxide (GO)
As one of the most studied 2D materials, graphene and graphene oxide have attracted much scientific interest.[38] Also, their application in membrane separation is very promising because of their perfect membrane formation by simply filtering the GO through filter papers.[39] Unlike the intrinsic pores inside CMSs and DLC, the porosity of GO membrane mainly results from the spaces between neighboring GO layers, namely, interlayer spacing. This interlayer spacing can be effectively controlled to accommodate the molecules with different sizes and thus precisely tune the selectivity, making the GO membrane very intriguing in the field of separation. Currently, the GO membranes are primarily used in aqueous solution such as sea water desalination, water softening, and small molecule separation in aqueous solution.[40] In organic solvent, Geim and co-workers[41] reported a highly laminated graphene oxide (HLGO) membrane fabricated by simple vacuum filtration of the HLGO layer (Figure  4). The ultrathin HLGO layer of $8\ \mathrm{nm}$ in thickness was successfully produced and showed excellent rejection performance with high organic solvent permeation. Compared with the commercial GO membrane, this HLGO membrane displayed ultrafast permeation performance for both water and different organic solvents. The ability of HLGO membrane for ultrafast liquid molecule permeation was attributed to the pinholes and very short (1 nm) 2D capillaries existed in the HLGO layer. This work demonstrates that the GO-based membrane might be a good candidate for OSN application.
## 4.2. Traditional Flexible Polymers
The traditional flexible polymers include 1D linear polymers and 3D crosslinked polymers. One of the most important characteristics of these polymers is the relatively dense packing of polymer chains and thus low mass transfer efficiency originated from their flexible backbones. Nevertheless, due to their feasibility of large-scale membrane fabrication via solution processing or interfacial polymerization, the flexible polymers have become dominated materials in the membrane separations since their introduction in the late 1970s.
## 4.2.1. Linear Polymers
Thanks to their excellent large-scale membrane formation feature, the soluble linear polymers such as poly(vinyl alcohol) (PVA), chitosan (CS), and poly(dimethylsiloxane) (PDMS) have been intensively used in industry for various separations since the last century such as sea water desalination, gas separation, alcohol/ water separation, and so on.[42] The biggest drawback of the linear polymer membranes is their dense structures without any permanent micropores, which greatly inhibit the efficient mass transfer through them. The mass transfer mechanism in these membranes is best described by the solution–diffusion model, in which the molecules first dissolve in the polymer matrix and then diffuse through them. To achieve the permanent micropores in these dense polymer membranes to improve the mass transfer efficiency, a plausible strategy is to incorporate various porous nanoparticles into these membranes to prepare a mix matrix membrane (MMM), which is discussed in Section 4.4.1.
## 4.2.2. Crosslinked Polymers
Another class of polymers broadly used for membrane separation is crosslinked polymers.[43] Compared with the linear ones, the crosslinked polymers are more resistant to organic solvents and are thus better choices for separation applications in organic solvent. Nowadays, the most popular crosslinked polymer is polyamide (PA).[44] Since the middle of the last century, PA ultrathin layer synthesized by interfacial polymerization on porous support has been utilized for seawater desalination. Subsequently, the composite membranes based on PA ultrathin separating layer have been investigated for different separation applications including nanofiltration, reverse osmosis, and so on. Recently, several excellent works related to PA membranes for ion removal in water system have been reported. The precise regulation of the micromorphology of the membrane by adjusting the membrane formation conditions is still of great importance for improving the membrane performance.[45] Most recently, Livingston and co-workers reported the PA-based composite membranes for OSN.[27a] In their study, controlled interfacial polymerization was used for preparing freestanding ultrathin PA membrane with thickness down to sub-10 nano­ meter. Due to the ultrathin thickness, the membrane with crumple microstructure exhibited the excellent separation performance with permeance up to $112\ensuremath{\mathrm{~L~m~}}^{-2}\ensuremath{\mathrm{~h~}}^{-1}\ensuremath{\mathrm{~bar}}$ −1 for acetonitrile, which was 2 orders of magnitude higher than traditional membranes with similar selectivity.
## 4.3. Semirigid Amorphous Materials
There is no doubt that increasing the membrane porosity would greatly improve the separation performance especially the solvent permeance, so the polymers with intrinsic permanent micropores have been prepared and their applications in the membrane separation field have been intensively studied, showing the unique separation performance compared to the dense membranes without permanent micropores. Several amorphous polymers with moderate backbone rigidity and relatively high porosity are developed by either introducing rigid monomers into the traditional flexible polymers or directly creating the semirigid backbones.
## 4.3.1. Polymers of Intrinsic Microporosity (PIMs)
PIMs are defined as the polymers providing a continuous network of interconnected voids, which are caused by the contorted shape and rigid backbone of polymers.[46] It was first reported by McKeown and co-workers in 2004 and has received a great deal of attention as a separating layer for thin-film composite membranes in gas separations and pervaporation applications.[47] The $5,5^{\prime},6,6^{\prime}$ -tetrahydroxy- $^{3,3,3^{\prime},3^{\prime}}$ -tetramethyl$^{1,1^{\prime}}$ -spirobisindane is the most used monomer for various PIM synthesis,[48] and other more rigid monomers are also designed and adopted to further improve the structural stability and microporosity of PIMs. McKeown and co-workers[49] fabricated a novel PIM polymer by replacing spirobisindane and dioxane units with more rigid ethanoanthracene (EA) and Tröger’s base (TB) to produce PIM–EA–TB. This polymer could be dissolved in organic solvent and showed good solution processability, leading to formation of the robust and defect-free PIM membrane by casting. The contorted and rigid structure of PIM provided both high porosity and pore interconnectivity, causing the outstanding performance for molecular sieving. For instance, the PIM membrane exhibited high permeability as well as excellent selectivity for different gas separations including hydrogen/oxygen and nitrogen/methane mixtures. Most recently, Livingston and co-workers[50] reported fabrication of PIM-1 membrane by simply casting the PIM-1 solution on glass substrate (Figure  5a). The PIM-1 membrane was peeled off from the substrate and transferred onto the porous support to form the composite membranes. By controlling the concentrations of casting solution, the thickness of PIM-1 membrane was adjusted from 35 to $660\ \mathrm{nm}$ . Due to the high porosity, these PIM-1 composite membranes showed the exceptional high performance for organic solvent nanofiltration. With the $140~\mathrm{nm}$ thick membrane, the permeance of $n$ -heptane was up $18~\mathrm{L~m^{-2}~h^{-1}~b a r^{-1}}$ , which was 90 times higher than the commercial polyimide membrane with similar selectivity. This work demonstrates that PIM membranes with relative rigid skeleton have great potential for membrane separation in organic solvent. It needs to be pointed out that the mass transfer efficiency of PIM membranes decreased when the membrane thickness is less than $140~\mathrm{nm}$ , which is attributed to the structural relaxation of non-crosslinking 1D chains of PIMs.
## 4.3.2. Crosslinked Polymers with Contorted Monomers
Compared with the linear polymers, the crosslinked polymers possess much higher structural stability, which effectively impedes the deformation of polymer chains. Unfortunately, the densely packed structure of traditional crosslinked polymer membranes prevents the gas or liquid pass through. To solve this problem, Livingston and co-workers[27b] fabricated the highly permeable polymer membranes by introducing rigid monomers into traditional crosslinked polymers (Figure  5b). Due to the introduction of rigid and contorted monomers, both the microporosity and pore interconnectivity were greatly enhanced. Compared with the polymer membranes with noncontorted planar monomers, the membranes with contorted monomers displayed the excellent mass transfer efficiency for organic solvent, with permeability of polyarylate membranes 2 orders of magnitude higher than traditional polyamide membrane. The simulation of polyarylate membranes with contorted monomers further revealed the relationship between the solvent permeance and the intermolecular voids at the molecular scale. This work shows that polymers with rigid and contorted molecular structures own the high porosity and pore interconnectivity, and thus have enormous potential for application in membrane separation.
## 4.3.3. Conjugated Microporous Polymers (CMPs)
CMPs are a class of organic porous polymers that combine $\pi$ -conjugated skeletons with permanent micropores.[51] Since the report of the first CMP by Cooper and co-workers in 2007,[52] various chemical reactions and building blocks have been developed to synthesize various CMPs with different chemical structures and specific properties have been synthesized.[53] Unlike the crystalline structure with ordered pores of COFs, CMPs are a class of amorphous materials that formed by irreversible C–C coupling reactions. Since no reversible chemical bonds exists, the chemical stability of CMPs is much higher than COFs. Despite the amorphous structure, the all-conjugated skeleton of CMP affords enough rigidity and high microporosity. The high chemical stability and microporosity make CMPs very attractive candidates for separation in organic medium.[54] However, the synthesized CMPs are typically insoluble powders, making them very challenging to form membranes. Cooper and coworkers[53b] recently prepared a soluble CMP by a two-step Suzuki cross-coupling reaction, which allowed for solutionprocessable membrane preparation. This is important step from the application perspective although the membrane is not stable in most organic solvents. Lindemann et al.[55] synthesized a freestanding CMP membrane by a layer-by-layer method. Defect-free membranes with thickness of several nanometers were obtained after etching the sacrificial layer. Compared with the traditional polymers, the prepared CMP membranes exhibited excellent gas selectivity, indicating the possibility of synthesizing CMP ultrathin membrane for separation, sensing and catalysis. Inspired by the surface polymerization of 1D polymers, our group recently reported surface polymerization of CMPs on the $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ surface modified by initial reaction sites (Figure 6).[56] By finely controlling the surface modification and polymerization conditions, defect-free membranes with square centimeter size were successfully obtained. The organic solvent nanofiltration testing showed excellent retention of solutes and broad-spectrum nanofiltration in both nonpolar hexane and polar methanol, with permeance 2 orders of magnitude higher than the commercial membranes. This high solvent permeance was mainly ascribed to the permanent micropores and high porosity of conjugated microporous polymers originated from the high rigidity of all-conjugated skeletons. Nevertheless, there is still a long way to go for the real industrial applications of CMP membranes. The key point is how to realize the low-cost, large-area, and defect-free production of such membranes.
## 4.4. Highly Rigid Crystalline Materials
Despite the intrinsic microporosity of the amorphous polymers with semirigid chemical structures, the pore uniformity is still poor. To achieve both high porosity and pore uniformity, several highly rigid crystalline materials, e.g., MOFs, COFs, and porous organic cages (POCs), have been developed. Noteworthily, high degree of crystallinity is a double-edged sword that improves pore uniformity while reducing membrane processability.
## 4.4.1. Metal–Organic Frameworks
MOFs are a class of highly crystalized hybrid materials, which are formed by coordination between inorganic metal nodes and organic linkers.[57] Thanks to the diversity of metal ions and organic monomers, MOFs possess unique chemical versatility and high structural flexibility.[58] As a highly porous powder materials, MOFs are primarily explored for small molecule adsorption with their applications in catalysts and sensors.[59] Although being difficult to form the continuous membranes, MOFs have received much attention in membrane separations by either pure MOFs or mixture of MOF particles and conventional polymers.[60] Their applications for membrane separation such as gas separation, pervaporation, water treatment, and OSN have also been greatly developed. In 2014, Yang and co-workers[61] reported an MOF membrane fabricated by vacuum filtration of 2D MOF nanosheets on porous substrate for gas separation. The ultrathin selective MOF layer had the abundant pore structures and showed ultrahigh permeability for hydrogen. For $\mathrm{\H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ separation, the permeance of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ was up to several thousand gas permeation units with high selectivity. Moreover, desalination through MOF membranes, as an emerging separation technique, is investigated by many researchers. For instance, Li and co-workers[62] prepared defectfree UiO-66 MOF membrane on porous alumina support via in situ solvothermal synthesis. This MOF membrane exhibited high rejection of multivalent ions with enhanced permeability. This improved rejection and permeance were ascribed to the existence of abundant micropores and interconnected channels in MOF membranes. Furthermore, this MOF membrane displayed good water and chemical stability, which suggested that it could be a potential candidate for water treatment. In addition to synthesize continuous membrane, MOF nanoparticles are also used as fillers in nanocomposite membrane, namely, the mix matrix membrane, for advanced separations. As an example, Livingston and co-workers[63] synthesized mix matrix membrane by incorporating different MOF particles with traditional PA membrane via interfacial polymerization (Figure  7a). Upon optimization of the synthesis conditions, four types of MOF particles including ZIF-8, MIL-53(Al), $\mathrm{NH}_{2}.$ - MIL-53(Al), and MIL-101(Cr) were homogeneously dispersed in PA membranes. The synthesized mix matrix membranes imbedded with MOF particles exhibited high mass transfer efficiency when compared with the original PA membranes. Impressively, the methanol flux of the membrane containing MIL-101(Cr) particles was more than 2 times higher than that of the original membrane, and the permeance of organic solvent might be further improved by increasing the MOF loading. This work manifests that the separation performance of conventional dense polymer membrane can be greatly enhanced by introducing the permanent micropores, and the new generation of thin film nanocomposite membranes will have great potential in OSN application.
## 4.4.2. Covalent Organic Frameworks (COFs)
COFs are an emerging class of crystalline porous organic structures, which backbones are composed of only light elements and linked by the robust covalent bonds.[64] The first type of COF is boronic ester COF reported by Yaghi and coworker in 2005, which was formed by the reversible reactions between phenyl diboronic acid and hexahydroxytriphenylene to give the periodic crystalline structures.[65] The crystalline structures offer this material the rigid porous frameworks, with pore size in the range of sub-nanometer to several nanometers. The rigid chemical structure of COFs consisting of organic covalent bonds endows themselves good thermal stability, ultrahigh specific surface area and permanent micropores.[66] However, due to their poor water stability, the boronic ester COFs have been mostly used in gas adsorption. In order to overcome the drawback of poor chemical stability of boronic ester COF, more stable Schiff base and triazine-based COFs have been synthesized and used for membrane separation in liquid phase.[67] Banerjee and co-workers[68] pioneered the synthesis of chemical stable COFs by modified Schiff base reaction, where the proton tautomerism resulted in the exceptional stable frameworks toward water, acid, and base. Afterward, the defect-free COF membranes were fabricated by both baking method[68b] and interfacial polymerization,[68a] and used for nanofiltration (Figure  7b). The pore structure and thickness of COF membranes were adjusted via changing monomer concentrations or casting conditions. As-prepared COF membranes showed good chemical stability and high mass transfer efficiency for organic solvents and could separate small organic molecules with molecular weight no more than 1000. These works indicate a hopeful way for scale-up synthesis of COF membranes with well-defined structures, which are important for future practical applications.
## 4.4.3. Porous Organic Cages
POCs are the individual molecules containing molecular level caves, which was first synthesized in 2009.[69] Different from the infinitely extended frameworks of MOFs and COFs, POCs are isolated molecules without any chemical crosslink between different cages. Notably, POCs are able to self-aggregate and form the large crystalline structures with surface area as high as $3000~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . The unique property of POCs is that they are soluble in organic solvents, allowing for solution processable membrane preparation.[70] However, owing to their high solubility in the common organic solvents, POCs are mainly used in gas or chiral molecule separation rather than OSN.[71] Cooper and co-workers[71b] synthesized porous organic cage CC3 via reaction of 1,3,5-triformylbenzene and 1,2-diaminocyclohexane. The prepared POC was successfully used for separating rare gas including krypton, xenon, and radon from air at a very low concentration. Moreover, they synthesized chiral CC3 with a homochiral diamine ((R,R)-1,2-cyclohexanediamine or (S,S)-1,2-cyclohexanediamine), showing outstanding separation performance toward chiral 1-phenylethanol. Recently, a defect-free POC membrane with thickness down to $50\ \mathrm{nm}$ was fabricated by simply spin coating of POCs in organic solvents.[72] The membrane structure was easily controlled by altering the processing conditions. The prepared POC membranes exhibited good chemical stability and selectivity for gas separation (Figure  8). However, the gas permeability of POC membrane decreased due to densification of POC cages. This work provides a feasible method for POC membrane fabrication and opens the door for their application in the membrane separation field.
## 5. Conclusion and Perspective
As an emerging technology, membrane separation in organic system has a huge potential for reducing the energy usage involved with the separation or purification processes in chemical industry. The successful development of organic solvent stable membranes extends the scope of applications of membrane separation from gas or aqueous system to organic liquid. The emerging highly porous materials with controllable pore size and pore surface will undoubtedly improve both the selectivity and solvent permeation, which will be the game changer compared with the conventional dense polymer membranes. Nevertheless, grand challenges are remaining for the future applications of membrane separation in organic solvents.
## 5.1. Membrane Stability
A significant number of promising materials have been produced, but very few can keep long-term stability in aggressive organic solvents. The general strategy to improve the structural stability of traditional polymer membranes is crosslinking; however, additional crosslinking steps are needed and solvent swelling still happens to some extent. Although the structural stability of the new developed porous materials is much more improved because of the more rigid 3D frameworks, some of them suffer from poor chemical stability due to their weak or reversible chemical bonds, such as MOF and COF. Therefore, the stability of membranes is a long-term topic especially when they are used in organic solvent.
## 5.2. Selectivity–Permeance Tradeoff
In fact, all the membrane separation processes have long been challenged by the tradeoff between selectivity and permeance. In organic system, $1{-}2~\mathrm{nm}$ pores are needed for OSN, which can separate large solutes from solvents with relative high solvent permeance; while less than $1\mathrm{nm}$ pores are needed for separating the similarly sized solvents, such as azeotropes or isomers, however with low permeance. It is obvious that pore size is of paramount importance for the selectivity and permeance of a membrane. Therefore, it is highly desirable to control the membrane selectivity via precisely pore size tuning. In addition, unlike the clear and direct correlation between pore size and membrane selectivity, the effect of pore surface on the selectivity and permeance is much more complicated and need to be intensively studied in the future. Indeed, the surface effect has been widely used in aqueous nanofiltration, in which the electrostatic repulsion between charged pore surface and ions dominates the ion selectivity. Nevertheless, the surface effect on the membrane selectivity in organic solvent has been seldom reported. Looking forward, future works would be to take full use of both pore size and pore surface to achieve the desirable selectivity and solvent permeance simultaneously.
## 5.3. Separation Mechanism
Understanding the separation mechanisms will certainly provide the guideline for rational design and synthesis of best materials with remarkable separation performance. In OSN, the selectivity originates from pore-size exclusion; whereas in OSRO, the separation is primarily determined by the sorption–diffusion model and the molecules are separated due to their different sorption behavior in the membrane. Nevertheless, for more challenging separations of very similar molecules such as $p$ -xylene and $\textit{o}$ -xylene, the selectivity from pore size and sorption difference is usually insufficient. Most recently, Koros and Zhang introduced so-called entropic selectivity, which might separate very similar molecules base on their shape difference.[1b] To achieve the high selectivity for different separations, a very important direction is to design and synthesize membranes that can separate molecules base on their different physiochemical properties, such as size, shape, polarity, dipole moment, condensing temperature, etc.
## 5.4. Scalable Membrane Fabrication
Compared with those in aqueous solution, membrane separation in organic solvent is still in its very infancy. Regardless of the big successes in new material design and high separation performance achieved, most of the membranes reported in the literatures are only limited for lab-scale studies with membrane size in several square centimeters. However, an industry membrane plant will need a typical membrane of few thousand to a million square meters. To date, only polymeric membranes have been made at this scale. Therefore, new technologies are urgently required for the large-scale membrane fabrication with reasonable cost, especially for the new developed microporous materials with outstanding performance like MOFs, COFs, CMPs, and so on.
